1
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:41,668
Help!

2
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:48,675
Eddie!

3
00:01:49,008 --> 00:01:50,076
What?

4
00:01:50,143 --> 00:01:51,444
Eddie, wake up!

5
00:01:52,011 --> 00:01:54,080
Don't move. Listen to me.

6
00:01:54,147 --> 00:01:56,683
Whatever you do, don't lean forward.

7
00:01:56,749 --> 00:01:58,384
-What's happening?
-No!

8
00:02:06,926 --> 00:02:07,961
<i>Hello.</i>

9
00:02:09,496 --> 00:02:10,830
<i>I want to play a game.</i>

10
00:02:12,131 --> 00:02:14,467
<i>The devices on your heads symbolize...</i>

11
00:02:14,534 --> 00:02:17,370
<i>the shackles you place upon others.</i>

12
00:02:17,737 --> 00:02:19,873
<i>You recklessly loaned money to people...</i>

13
00:02:20,139 --> 00:02:22,108
<i>knowing their financial limitations.</i>

14
00:02:22,375 --> 00:02:26,579
<i>Counting on repossessing more than they could</i>
<i>ever pay back.</i>

15
00:02:27,313 --> 00:02:29,382
<i>You are predators,</i>

16
00:02:29,449 --> 00:02:31,851
<i>but today, you become the prey,</i>

17
00:02:31,918 --> 00:02:35,522
<i>and you will pay the price I demand with your</i>
<i>flesh.</i>

18
00:02:35,588 --> 00:02:36,689
This is your fault!

19
00:02:36,756 --> 00:02:38,424
<i>The scale before you...</i>

20
00:02:38,491 --> 00:02:39,626
<i>is your only path to freedom.</i>

21
00:02:39,893 --> 00:02:43,129
<i>However, only one of you may leave,</i>

22
00:02:43,796 --> 00:02:46,866
<i>and the toll is the ultimate sacrifice:</i>

23
00:02:47,433 --> 00:02:49,502
<i>The sacrifice of flesh.</i>

24
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:52,171
<i>Before you are the instruments...</i>

25
00:02:52,438 --> 00:02:54,007
<i>to obtain that flesh.</i>

26
00:02:54,741 --> 00:02:59,312
<i>Move with haste, for when the 60 seconds are</i>
<i>up...</i>

27
00:02:59,579 --> 00:03:02,081
<i>the one who has given the most flesh...</i>

28
00:03:02,348 --> 00:03:04,217
<i>will have fulfilled their end of the bargain,</i>

29
00:03:04,284 --> 00:03:06,419
<i>while the device on your opponent's head...</i>

30
00:03:06,486 --> 00:03:09,322
<i>will engage and drill into their skull.</i>

31
00:03:09,689 --> 00:03:11,724
<i>Who will offer the most flesh...</i>

32
00:03:12,025 --> 00:03:13,993
<i>to save their life?</i>

33
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:15,428
<i>The choice is yours.</i>

34
00:03:28,675 --> 00:03:30,543
I'm not dying because of you, bitch!

35
00:05:57,523 --> 00:05:59,358
<i>I ask you, Special Agent Strahm:</i>

36
00:06:01,894 --> 00:06:03,696
<i>Have you learned enough to trust me?</i>

37
00:06:03,963 --> 00:06:06,032
<i>For if you have not, this room...</i>

38
00:06:06,099 --> 00:06:09,235
<i>will become your tomb, and you will never be</i>
<i>heard from again.</i>

39
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:14,006
<i>Your body will never be found, and my legacy</i>
<i>will become yours.</i>

40
00:06:15,341 --> 00:06:16,743
<i>Make your choice.</i>

41
00:07:45,932 --> 00:07:47,233
Hey, baby.

42
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:48,401
Well, listen.

43
00:07:48,467 --> 00:07:51,237
I'm so sorry, but I can't make dinner.

44
00:07:53,272 --> 00:07:54,574
I'll be working late again.

45
00:07:55,074 --> 00:07:56,709
I have a meeting with legal counsel,

46
00:07:56,776 --> 00:07:59,779
and it's going to keep me busy for the rest of
the afternoon.

47
00:08:03,216 --> 00:08:06,252
I know it's your birthday, and I'll make it up
to you. I promise.

48
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:12,225
I love you.

49
00:08:15,061 --> 00:08:17,630
-What were we talking about?
-Your deposition.

50
00:08:18,564 --> 00:08:19,832
Then don't worry about me, Debbie.

51
00:08:19,899 --> 00:08:21,701
Some small-time lawyer trying to squeeze us...

52
00:08:21,968 --> 00:08:23,970
is not going to make me say anything I don't
want to.

53
00:08:24,036 --> 00:08:26,739
I have no doubt, but as lead counsel for this
company,

54
00:08:26,806 --> 00:08:28,741
if you make a mistake, I'm the one who pays
for it,

55
00:08:29,375 --> 00:08:31,310
so what do you say we prepare your deposition?

56
00:08:31,611 --> 00:08:33,112
Oh, Christ.

57
00:08:33,179 --> 00:08:35,181
Do you remember meeting with Mr. Harold
Abbott?

58
00:08:35,448 --> 00:08:37,650
William, I have a Casey Patterson on line one.

59
00:08:37,717 --> 00:08:38,885
Take a message, Addy.

60
00:08:38,951 --> 00:08:40,753
I'll call her back this week.

61
00:08:41,487 --> 00:08:43,789
Someday. Go on.

62
00:08:43,856 --> 00:08:45,224
When opposing counsel...

63
00:08:45,291 --> 00:08:46,292
asks you about Mr. Abbott,

64
00:08:46,359 --> 00:08:47,360
what will you say?

65
00:08:47,426 --> 00:08:50,162
I'll say that, as vice president...

66
00:08:50,496 --> 00:08:52,899
of Claims and Adjustments, my job is...

67
00:08:52,965 --> 00:08:55,101
to review the cancellation of insurance
policies.

68
00:08:55,368 --> 00:08:56,802
In fact, Mr. Abbott was sitting...

69
00:08:57,069 --> 00:08:59,005
right where you are now when we discussed his
claim.

70
00:09:01,507 --> 00:09:03,643
I just don't understand.

71
00:09:03,709 --> 00:09:06,412
I've been a customer of this insurance company
for over ten years.

72
00:09:06,679 --> 00:09:09,882
I know, but unfortunately, when we reviewed
your claim,

73
00:09:09,949 --> 00:09:12,485
we discovered you failed to disclose a
pre-existing condition.

74
00:09:12,985 --> 00:09:15,021
What condition? I don't have any condition.

75
00:09:16,722 --> 00:09:18,758
It says here you had oral surgery...

76
00:09:18,824 --> 00:09:20,660
to remove a cyst from your jaw.

77
00:09:20,726 --> 00:09:22,728
That's absurd! I have a heart condition.

78
00:09:22,795 --> 00:09:24,163
That has nothing to do with oral surgery...

79
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:25,564
that I had thirty years ago!

80
00:09:25,831 --> 00:09:28,601
Any type of oral surgery leaves scar tissue.

81
00:09:28,868 --> 00:09:30,703
Scar tissue can lead to periodontal disease,

82
00:09:30,770 --> 00:09:31,804
and as you know,

83
00:09:31,871 --> 00:09:33,773
periodontal disease can lead to heart disease.

84
00:09:34,941 --> 00:09:36,876
You know what? You're a criminal.

85
00:09:39,178 --> 00:09:41,547
A goddamn criminal.

86
00:09:42,715 --> 00:09:44,383
I paid my premiums for ten years,

87
00:09:44,650 --> 00:09:46,152
when all I ever had was a cold,

88
00:09:46,552 --> 00:09:47,820
and now that I'm actually sick,

89
00:09:47,887 --> 00:09:49,388
you're denying my coverage?

90
00:09:49,455 --> 00:09:51,958
-I have a family!
-Those are the rules, Harold!

91
00:09:52,558 --> 00:09:55,194
I'm sorry, but your own actions led to this.

92
00:09:57,630 --> 00:09:59,298
You just gave me a death sentence.

93
00:09:59,565 --> 00:10:01,467
Now who's going to cover me?

94
00:10:02,335 --> 00:10:03,970
You just killed me.

95
00:10:06,639 --> 00:10:08,074
I have a family too,

96
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:09,942
so I can empathize with your argument,

97
00:10:10,009 --> 00:10:11,777
but essentially, you lied on your application.

98
00:10:12,044 --> 00:10:14,080
-Wait. Be careful, Will.
-What?

99
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:16,182
Do you think he did it on purpose?

100
00:10:20,086 --> 00:10:23,222
It wasn't my job to assess his intentions,

101
00:10:23,289 --> 00:10:25,758
but to verify the accuracy of his claim.

102
00:10:26,025 --> 00:10:27,860
Everybody thinks we're the bad guys.

103
00:10:28,127 --> 00:10:29,495
Nobody mentions the millions of people...

104
00:10:29,762 --> 00:10:31,664
we help every year without incident.

105
00:10:31,731 --> 00:10:35,201
Or the millions of dollars we donate to
charity.

106
00:10:35,267 --> 00:10:37,336
Or the free clinics we help fund...

107
00:10:37,403 --> 00:10:39,105
Short answers, Will. Short answers.

108
00:10:39,171 --> 00:10:41,841
<i>The estate of one of history's...</i>

109
00:10:42,108 --> 00:10:43,843
<i>most notorious serial killers, John Kramer,</i>

110
00:10:44,110 --> 00:10:45,344
<i>was distributed today.</i>

111
00:10:45,411 --> 00:10:47,146
<i>It consisted mostly of real estate,</i>

112
00:10:47,413 --> 00:10:49,015
<i>but in the time I've been investigating...</i>

113
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:50,082
One more question:

114
00:10:50,149 --> 00:10:51,517
Who found the discrepancy on his application?

115
00:10:52,284 --> 00:10:53,486
The bloodhounds.

116
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:56,122
They work as a team.

117
00:10:57,223 --> 00:10:59,425
If there's a discrepancy in an application,

118
00:10:59,959 --> 00:11:01,360
the six of them will find it.

119
00:11:01,694 --> 00:11:03,295
Sir, they're waiting for you in the conference
room.

120
00:11:03,963 --> 00:11:05,231
All right.

121
00:11:05,297 --> 00:11:06,665
How's everybody doing today?

122
00:11:09,535 --> 00:11:11,637
I found two errors on a customer's
application.

123
00:11:12,104 --> 00:11:14,306
This guy practically lives at the doctor's
office.

124
00:11:14,607 --> 00:11:17,877
This'll save us about two hundred thousand
dollars.

125
00:11:17,943 --> 00:11:20,046
-The Terminator strikes again.
-I'll be back in a couple of hours.

126
00:11:21,614 --> 00:11:23,049
Take care, Hank.

127
00:11:25,551 --> 00:11:27,520
<i>the one who has given the most flesh,</i>

128
00:11:27,586 --> 00:11:29,722
<i>to save their life...</i>

129
00:11:29,789 --> 00:11:31,290
<i>The choice is yours.</i>

130
00:11:31,624 --> 00:11:33,759
<i>I'm not dying because of you, bitch!</i>

131
00:11:44,070 --> 00:11:45,037
Yeah.

132
00:11:46,872 --> 00:11:48,240
I'll be right there.

133
00:12:02,788 --> 00:12:03,889
What's going on?

134
00:12:03,956 --> 00:12:05,191
The FBI has taken over the crime scene.

135
00:12:05,257 --> 00:12:06,425
They want you down there as soon as possible.

136
00:12:18,804 --> 00:12:20,106
Erickson.

137
00:12:21,307 --> 00:12:23,476
I didn't know you could leave your desk.

138
00:12:23,909 --> 00:12:25,344
I make exceptions when we find...

139
00:12:25,611 --> 00:12:27,179
fingerprints at a Jigsaw crime scene.

140
00:12:29,982 --> 00:12:31,250
Take a look.

141
00:12:33,853 --> 00:12:35,187
We also got a right index print,

142
00:12:35,254 --> 00:12:36,188
off the scale.

143
00:12:37,389 --> 00:12:39,091
-Have they been identified?
-Yes.

144
00:12:40,126 --> 00:12:41,494
They're Agent Strahm's.

145
00:12:42,528 --> 00:12:45,397
When I knew he and Perez were being targeted
by Jigsaw,

146
00:12:46,465 --> 00:12:47,933
I should have been more vigilant,

147
00:12:48,534 --> 00:12:50,102
but I never thought this could happen.

148
00:12:50,469 --> 00:12:51,737
Not from Peter Strahm.

149
00:12:52,004 --> 00:12:53,572
It surprised all of us.

150
00:12:53,639 --> 00:12:58,878
Yeah, but we have something he doesn't know
about.

151
00:13:01,247 --> 00:13:02,581
Follow me.

152
00:13:08,954 --> 00:13:09,889
Lindsey.

153
00:13:14,226 --> 00:13:15,661
Detective Hoffman.

154
00:13:16,162 --> 00:13:17,363
Perez?

155
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:23,302
It was my decision, Detective.

156
00:13:23,369 --> 00:13:25,204
I knew Jigsaw wasn't working alone,

157
00:13:25,271 --> 00:13:26,572
but until I knew for sure who was helping
him...

158
00:13:26,639 --> 00:13:29,074
I couldn't guarantee her safety.

159
00:13:29,942 --> 00:13:31,810
So you let me believe she was dead?

160
00:13:31,877 --> 00:13:33,379
Well, I didn't know who I could trust.

161
00:13:36,081 --> 00:13:37,750
What else have you been hiding from me?

162
00:13:38,617 --> 00:13:39,852
We know Strahm was familiar with...

163
00:13:39,919 --> 00:13:41,720
the five involved in the real-estate scam,

164
00:13:41,987 --> 00:13:44,056
the victims in the traps Erickson found.

165
00:13:44,123 --> 00:13:45,558
-How do you know?
-Strahm and I investigated them...

166
00:13:45,858 --> 00:13:48,394
after the fire. It was arson.

167
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:49,895
The five of them were responsible,

168
00:13:50,162 --> 00:13:52,097
but after our only witness disappeared,

169
00:13:52,164 --> 00:13:53,532
no charges were ever filed.

170
00:13:54,466 --> 00:13:55,801
Strahm couldn't let them get away with it,

171
00:13:56,068 --> 00:13:57,269
so he put them in a trap...

172
00:13:57,336 --> 00:13:59,271
where killing each other was their only way
out.

173
00:13:59,572 --> 00:14:01,273
So what are you saying? Vigilante justice?

174
00:14:01,540 --> 00:14:02,841
Call it whatever you want.

175
00:14:03,309 --> 00:14:04,577
But we have to find him.

176
00:14:09,281 --> 00:14:10,683
We'd like to work together on this.

177
00:14:12,151 --> 00:14:14,153
You led me to believe she was dead,

178
00:14:14,420 --> 00:14:15,588
and now you want to work with me?

179
00:14:16,021 --> 00:14:18,090
Look, we're offering full disclosure,
Detective,

180
00:14:18,157 --> 00:14:19,592
so from now on, whatever we know,

181
00:14:19,658 --> 00:14:20,793
you'll know.

182
00:14:21,093 --> 00:14:22,361
Does that sound fair?

183
00:14:31,370 --> 00:14:33,439
Detective Hoffman, can we talk?

184
00:14:33,505 --> 00:14:36,442
Pamela Jenkins, the Jigsaw sensationalist.

185
00:14:36,508 --> 00:14:38,711
-I only report the facts.
-Is that so?

186
00:14:38,777 --> 00:14:42,414
"John Kramer: The Butcher's Mystery."

187
00:14:42,481 --> 00:14:43,482
If you'd like to make an official statement,

188
00:14:43,549 --> 00:14:45,184
I'd be more than happy to take it.

189
00:14:45,517 --> 00:14:47,386
You twist the facts to make a better story.

190
00:14:47,653 --> 00:14:48,854
It's irresponsible.

191
00:14:49,421 --> 00:14:51,357
Excuse me. Nice meeting you.

192
00:14:51,690 --> 00:14:53,459
I know more about John Kramer than you think.

193
00:14:55,694 --> 00:14:59,531
-I doubt it.
-He left his wife a box.

194
00:14:59,598 --> 00:15:01,967
-Did you know that?
-Maybe, maybe not.

195
00:15:02,801 --> 00:15:04,637
-How do you know?
-Probate court.

196
00:15:04,703 --> 00:15:06,639
It's public record. You just have to know
where to look.

197
00:15:07,339 --> 00:15:09,608
All right. What do you really want?

198
00:15:09,675 --> 00:15:11,877
Jill Tuck. Help me find her,

199
00:15:11,944 --> 00:15:13,879
and I'll tone down the sensationalism.

200
00:15:14,847 --> 00:15:15,881
Really?

201
00:15:17,283 --> 00:15:18,684
I'll see what I can do.

202
00:15:30,796 --> 00:15:32,197
I don't know how I got there.

203
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:35,934
I just opened my eyes...

204
00:15:36,935 --> 00:15:38,470
He did this to me.

205
00:15:38,904 --> 00:15:40,272
Who did?

206
00:15:41,907 --> 00:15:43,175
Jigsaw.

207
00:15:43,676 --> 00:15:45,244
Didn't you cut off your own arm?

208
00:15:46,078 --> 00:15:47,980
I did. I did.

209
00:15:48,714 --> 00:15:50,349
But he made me do it.

210
00:15:51,550 --> 00:15:52,951
Why?

211
00:15:53,285 --> 00:15:56,488
Because what Eddie and I were doing was wrong.

212
00:15:59,525 --> 00:16:01,260
We were ruining people's lives.

213
00:16:06,398 --> 00:16:07,900
He wanted us to learn.

214
00:16:09,268 --> 00:16:10,469
And did you?

215
00:16:13,572 --> 00:16:14,873
Look at me!

216
00:16:16,675 --> 00:16:18,610
Look at my fucking arm!

217
00:16:19,345 --> 00:16:21,246
What the hell am I supposed to learn from
this?

218
00:16:22,448 --> 00:16:23,849
Look at my arm!

219
00:16:23,916 --> 00:16:26,585
What am I supposed to learn from this?

220
00:16:27,186 --> 00:16:28,554
Look at me!

221
00:16:35,527 --> 00:16:37,162
<i>Hello, Gideon!</i>

222
00:16:38,330 --> 00:16:39,665
<i>Good name, sweetheart.</i>

223
00:16:41,734 --> 00:16:44,570
<i>Could have been Scarlett, but no, it had to be</i>
<i>a boy.</i>

224
00:16:45,437 --> 00:16:46,872
<i>Let's see if we all fit.</i>

225
00:16:49,108 --> 00:16:53,145
<i>Come on, get closer. Here we go.</i>

226
00:16:55,547 --> 00:16:58,050
<i>-Hello, Gideon.</i>
<i>-This is a happy family.</i>

227
00:16:58,684 --> 00:17:00,686
<i>We love you, son. We're waiting for you.</i>

228
00:17:12,464 --> 00:17:14,333
<i>Gideon, Gideon, Gideon.</i>

229
00:17:15,501 --> 00:17:16,935
<i>Jill, if you're watching this,</i>

230
00:17:17,002 --> 00:17:18,771
<i>I've been gone from this world for quite some</i>
<i>time.</i>

231
00:17:20,339 --> 00:17:22,141
<i>I'm leaving you a box today,</i>

232
00:17:22,474 --> 00:17:25,944
<i>and its contents are of the utmost importance.</i>

233
00:18:04,817 --> 00:18:06,118
<i>I'm not home right now,</i>

234
00:18:06,185 --> 00:18:07,419
<i>but please leave a message.</i>

235
00:18:08,287 --> 00:18:09,621
<i>Ms. Tuck, this is Pamela Jenkins,</i>

236
00:18:09,888 --> 00:18:11,156
<i>calling once again.</i>

237
00:18:11,423 --> 00:18:13,559
<i>I hoped you could clear up a few things about</i>
<i>your husband.</i>

238
00:18:13,892 --> 00:18:17,796
<i>I found something very interesting about John</i>
<i>Kramer's death.</i>

239
00:18:19,298 --> 00:18:20,966
<i>Are you there, Detective?</i>

240
00:18:22,234 --> 00:18:23,902
<i>Upon hearing this tape,</i>

241
00:18:23,969 --> 00:18:26,505
<i>some will assume that it's over.</i>

242
00:18:28,073 --> 00:18:30,642
<i>You feel like you're in control, don't you?</i>

243
00:18:32,411 --> 00:18:35,547
<i>You think you'll get away without being</i>
<i>tested?</i>

244
00:18:40,552 --> 00:18:41,954
Look at this.

245
00:18:42,554 --> 00:18:44,022
These are the puzzle pieces...

246
00:18:44,089 --> 00:18:45,624
cut from his previous victims.

247
00:18:48,193 --> 00:18:50,596
This is the piece from the latest victim.

248
00:18:52,064 --> 00:18:54,733
-It's the same as the others.
-Yeah, that's what we thought.

249
00:18:55,434 --> 00:18:56,468
Abrasions on the skin,

250
00:18:56,735 --> 00:18:59,371
indicating a serrated knife or sharp object.

251
00:19:02,608 --> 00:19:04,810
-So?
-All the other cuts...

252
00:19:04,877 --> 00:19:06,979
were made with a surgical-grade blade.

253
00:19:10,616 --> 00:19:11,683
Obviously, Strahm used...

254
00:19:11,750 --> 00:19:12,985
a different knife than John Kramer.

255
00:19:13,051 --> 00:19:14,753
Right, but that got us curious,

256
00:19:14,820 --> 00:19:16,622
so we pulled the files to compare.

257
00:19:16,989 --> 00:19:19,224
That same knife was used on only one other
victim.

258
00:19:19,658 --> 00:19:21,493
And that victim was Seth Baxter,

259
00:19:23,295 --> 00:19:25,464
the man who killed your sister.

260
00:19:26,732 --> 00:19:28,600
You're telling me you can tell...

261
00:19:28,667 --> 00:19:30,435
a different knife was used just from a photo?

262
00:19:31,036 --> 00:19:32,504
No, but I can.

263
00:19:33,405 --> 00:19:35,040
I was the one who examined the body.

264
00:19:35,807 --> 00:19:38,010
I've examined every victim of the Jigsaw
Killer.

265
00:19:38,477 --> 00:19:39,444
Good work.

266
00:19:39,745 --> 00:19:40,913
The tape from the latest victim was missing,

267
00:19:41,179 --> 00:19:43,582
so we're looking into the Baxter tape.

268
00:19:44,082 --> 00:19:45,150
Why?

269
00:19:45,851 --> 00:19:49,988
If someone else cut the piece, maybe someone
else made the recording.

270
00:19:51,356 --> 00:19:53,759
-Strahm?
-Yeah, maybe.

271
00:19:54,393 --> 00:19:56,662
Strahm's voice on that tape would be our
smoking gun.

272
00:19:56,962 --> 00:20:00,065
If we had it, we could go public with his
involvement.

273
00:20:15,113 --> 00:20:18,750
Jane, it's all right, okay?

274
00:20:37,169 --> 00:20:39,037
I didn't expect to see you here so soon.

275
00:20:40,639 --> 00:20:41,974
Change of plans.

276
00:20:43,475 --> 00:20:44,977
The game begins tonight.

277
00:20:46,345 --> 00:20:47,412
Why?

278
00:20:47,479 --> 00:20:49,982
Because somebody knows about the box who
shouldn't.

279
00:20:50,048 --> 00:20:51,650
-Who?
-None of your business.

280
00:20:52,150 --> 00:20:53,785
All you need to know is that from now on,

281
00:20:54,286 --> 00:20:56,088
I control every aspect of the game.

282
00:20:57,589 --> 00:20:58,991
That's not what John wanted.

283
00:21:00,425 --> 00:21:03,161
Give me the envelopes. That's not a request.

284
00:21:14,239 --> 00:21:15,340
Here.

285
00:21:18,477 --> 00:21:19,978
Is that all there is?

286
00:21:20,679 --> 00:21:24,049
-Yes.
-From now on, I work alone.

287
00:21:24,916 --> 00:21:26,385
I know.

288
00:21:26,451 --> 00:21:28,754
I'm just carrying out John's final wishes.

289
00:21:29,021 --> 00:21:32,290
Well, John's dead. And his work is almost
done.

290
00:21:36,928 --> 00:21:39,297
-Who's that?
-Loose ends.

291
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:43,769
When I'm finished,

292
00:21:46,138 --> 00:21:47,939
we won't speak again.

293
00:22:02,954 --> 00:22:04,356
Methadone is a masking agent.

294
00:22:04,423 --> 00:22:06,825
It doesn't heal. It just numbs the senses.

295
00:22:08,627 --> 00:22:10,362
I've found a better way.

296
00:22:12,497 --> 00:22:13,999
These people...

297
00:22:14,566 --> 00:22:18,236
will keep hurting you and letting you down.

298
00:22:18,737 --> 00:22:21,273
They're addicts, John. Recovery is a process.

299
00:22:21,339 --> 00:22:22,340
Maybe addiction...

300
00:22:22,407 --> 00:22:23,575
is just part of human nature.

301
00:22:24,576 --> 00:22:26,178
But what about these people, Jill,

302
00:22:26,445 --> 00:22:29,114
who come here every day and use you?

303
00:22:29,948 --> 00:22:31,583
They bide their time.

304
00:22:31,950 --> 00:22:33,852
They're avoiding prison sentences.

305
00:22:34,753 --> 00:22:38,623
You hook them on methadone. You call that
recovery?

306
00:22:38,690 --> 00:22:42,094
-It's not that simple.
-Addiction isn't simple!

307
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:44,162
Jill! Wake up!

308
00:22:45,997 --> 00:22:50,969
These people have no respect for the lives
they destroy.

309
00:22:52,170 --> 00:22:54,740
Once you come face-to-face with death,

310
00:22:59,544 --> 00:23:01,613
then you'll know the value of life.

311
00:23:05,016 --> 00:23:06,718
That's my way,

312
00:23:08,153 --> 00:23:10,956
and I have proof it works.

313
00:23:18,630 --> 00:23:21,199
-Amanda?
-Hello, Jill.

314
00:23:24,369 --> 00:23:25,871
Jill,

315
00:23:27,806 --> 00:23:31,443
you once told me she was a lost soul.

316
00:23:34,045 --> 00:23:35,947
But here she stands.

317
00:23:36,715 --> 00:23:39,618
She's clean and whole.

318
00:23:41,953 --> 00:23:45,724
She has a new appreciation for her life.

319
00:23:46,558 --> 00:23:48,059
It works.

320
00:23:48,326 --> 00:23:50,796
It's real. He helped me.

321
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:03,708
Okay, John.

322
00:24:18,523 --> 00:24:20,292
<i>Although police would have you believe...</i>

323
00:24:20,358 --> 00:24:23,261
<i>that John Kramer, aka Jigsaw, is dead,</i>

324
00:24:23,528 --> 00:24:25,897
<i>they claimed to have discovered last week...</i>

325
00:24:25,964 --> 00:24:28,400
<i>a new game with horrifying implications.</i>

326
00:24:28,466 --> 00:24:29,868
<i>The victims were all connected...</i>

327
00:24:30,135 --> 00:24:32,971
<i>to the life of John Kramer, however remotely.</i>

328
00:24:33,638 --> 00:24:35,574
<i>The Jigsaw Killer may be dead,</i>

329
00:24:36,441 --> 00:24:38,210
<i>but the murders continue.</i>

330
00:24:47,586 --> 00:24:48,920
Shit!

331
00:24:52,657 --> 00:24:53,992
Hank, are you still here?

332
00:25:27,459 --> 00:25:28,493
Don't move!

333
00:25:37,068 --> 00:25:38,536
Shit!

334
00:25:38,603 --> 00:25:41,373
Oh, God! Okay, I'll take care of it.

335
00:25:41,840 --> 00:25:43,541
How'd you get in here? Are you one of us?

336
00:26:24,516 --> 00:26:25,650
<i>Hello, William.</i>

337
00:26:26,251 --> 00:26:27,252
Oh, God.

338
00:26:27,319 --> 00:26:30,388
<i>You probably wondered when we'd meet again.</i>

339
00:26:31,923 --> 00:26:33,925
-Today is that day.
-No!

340
00:26:34,392 --> 00:26:35,427
<i>For years,</i>

341
00:26:35,493 --> 00:26:38,830
<i>your probability formula has decided the fate</i>
<i>of others.</i>

342
00:26:39,364 --> 00:26:40,932
<i>The healthy benefited,</i>

343
00:26:40,999 --> 00:26:42,367
<i>while those who might become sick...</i>

344
00:26:42,434 --> 00:26:44,469
<i>were unjustly rejected.</i>

345
00:26:44,769 --> 00:26:46,905
<i>However, the formula fails to account for...</i>

346
00:26:47,472 --> 00:26:49,107
<i>the human will to live.</i>

347
00:26:49,441 --> 00:26:50,709
<i>When faced with death,</i>

348
00:26:50,976 --> 00:26:53,578
<i>who should live and who will live...</i>

349
00:26:54,145 --> 00:26:55,981
<i>are two entirely different things.</i>

350
00:26:56,247 --> 00:26:58,850
<i>Today, your policy will be put to the test.</i>

351
00:26:59,284 --> 00:27:01,720
<i>There are four straps around your limbs,</i>

352
00:27:01,786 --> 00:27:04,222
<i>and four tests you must complete.</i>

353
00:27:04,923 --> 00:27:06,257
<i>If you fail,</i>

354
00:27:06,324 --> 00:27:09,794
<i>the straps on your arms and legs will</i>
<i>detonate.</i>

355
00:27:12,731 --> 00:27:14,199
<i>Look closely.</i>

356
00:27:19,537 --> 00:27:20,472
<i>You have 60 minutes...</i>

357
00:27:20,739 --> 00:27:23,074
<i>to complete the tests and avoid this fate.</i>

358
00:27:23,475 --> 00:27:24,876
<i>Starting now.</i>

359
00:27:25,877 --> 00:27:27,912
<i>You are not alone in this game.</i>

360
00:27:27,979 --> 00:27:30,715
<i>Just as you took loved ones away from their</i>
<i>families,</i>

361
00:27:30,782 --> 00:27:33,485
<i>if you don't reach the end before the timer</i>
<i>runs out,</i>

362
00:27:33,551 --> 00:27:36,021
<i>you'll never see your family again.</i>

363
00:27:39,257 --> 00:27:42,894
Mom? Mom! Mom, wake up!

364
00:27:43,728 --> 00:27:44,963
Mom?

365
00:27:48,566 --> 00:27:49,934
Where are we?

366
00:27:51,002 --> 00:27:52,337
I don't know.

367
00:27:52,404 --> 00:27:53,872
-What's this?
-I don't know.

368
00:27:54,439 --> 00:27:55,673
Look.

369
00:28:00,712 --> 00:28:02,414
Oh, my God!

370
00:28:04,549 --> 00:28:06,751
<i>Here is your first test.</i>

371
00:28:07,318 --> 00:28:09,320
<i>Your health and genetic profile...</i>

372
00:28:09,654 --> 00:28:12,123
<i>put you in the highest category for success.</i>

373
00:28:12,524 --> 00:28:15,527
<i>However, the same cannot be said of your</i>
<i>opponent.</i>

374
00:28:19,564 --> 00:28:21,933
<i>At 52 years old,</i>

375
00:28:22,434 --> 00:28:25,303
<i>this man continued smoking even though he</i>
<i>knew...</i>

376
00:28:25,370 --> 00:28:27,205
<i>he had a history of high blood pressure...</i>

377
00:28:27,272 --> 00:28:28,873
<i>and heart disease.</i>

378
00:28:28,940 --> 00:28:33,378
<i>That shows how little regard he has for his</i>
<i>own life.</i>

379
00:28:33,912 --> 00:28:36,548
<i>Your game will focus on one simple element.</i>

380
00:28:37,315 --> 00:28:38,650
<i>Air.</i>

381
00:28:38,917 --> 00:28:42,487
<i>Once the game begins, every time you inhale...</i>

382
00:28:42,754 --> 00:28:45,290
<i>the clamps on your chests will tighten,</i>

383
00:28:45,557 --> 00:28:47,125
<i>crushing your bodies.</i>

384
00:28:47,392 --> 00:28:51,229
<i>The only way to escape is for the other to</i>
<i>fail.</i>

385
00:28:51,296 --> 00:28:53,331
<i>So I ask you, when facing death,</i>

386
00:28:53,731 --> 00:28:55,467
<i>who will survive?</i>

387
00:28:56,568 --> 00:28:58,236
<i>Live or die, William.</i>

388
00:28:59,003 --> 00:29:00,705
<i>Make your choice.</i>

389
00:29:01,239 --> 00:29:03,775
-Fuck you!
-Who the hell is that?

390
00:29:10,415 --> 00:29:11,483
Hold your breath!

391
00:31:01,859 --> 00:31:03,361
Shit!

392
00:31:03,861 --> 00:31:05,163
Jesus Christ!

393
00:31:37,962 --> 00:31:39,964
Come on! Come on!

394
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:50,108
Shit.

395
00:31:58,016 --> 00:32:01,986
Please help us! Help us, please!

396
00:32:02,687 --> 00:32:04,689
Please! Help us!

397
00:32:05,323 --> 00:32:06,958
Somebody, please!

398
00:32:08,526 --> 00:32:09,827
Please!

399
00:32:10,528 --> 00:32:12,997
Help! Anybody!

400
00:32:13,865 --> 00:32:16,434
"Live or die?" What the hell is this?

401
00:32:19,570 --> 00:32:20,705
It's connected to that thing.

402
00:32:27,412 --> 00:32:28,946
HF.

403
00:32:29,013 --> 00:32:31,015
-What does that mean?
-Hydrofluoric acid.

404
00:32:34,519 --> 00:32:37,855
This stuff can eat through skin in seconds.

405
00:32:38,523 --> 00:32:40,391
-What do we do?
-I don't know.

406
00:32:40,892 --> 00:32:42,393
You want to live, don't you?

407
00:32:42,694 --> 00:32:45,630
What about the timer? It must mean something.

408
00:32:46,998 --> 00:32:49,934
No idea. I don't know why we're here.

409
00:32:50,568 --> 00:32:52,236
We're here because of your father.

410
00:33:16,327 --> 00:33:19,063
WHAT YOU'RE LOOKING FOR: 545 4TH STREET,
APARTMENT 413

411
00:33:41,018 --> 00:33:43,788
-Miss Jenkins.
-This will only take a second.

412
00:33:44,355 --> 00:33:45,490
We can help each other.

413
00:33:45,556 --> 00:33:47,658
Why do you think you can just show up like
this?

414
00:33:47,725 --> 00:33:49,160
Because I found something you'll want to see.

415
00:33:51,662 --> 00:33:52,730
Go ahead.

416
00:33:57,668 --> 00:33:59,036
Where did you get this?

417
00:33:59,103 --> 00:34:00,805
It was at the place where John died.

418
00:34:01,105 --> 00:34:02,540
Does it mean anything to you?

419
00:34:03,107 --> 00:34:04,308
No.

420
00:34:04,842 --> 00:34:06,344
Goodbye, Miss Jenkins.

421
00:34:11,616 --> 00:34:13,017
If anything comes to mind...

422
00:35:14,011 --> 00:35:16,047
<i>I want to thank you for sponsoring this party,</i>
<i>William.</i>

423
00:35:16,113 --> 00:35:18,516
<i>And I know how much the clinic appreciates it.</i>

424
00:35:19,383 --> 00:35:21,953
-John Kramer.
-William Easton.

425
00:35:22,019 --> 00:35:23,888
Kramer? You're Jill's husband, aren't you?

426
00:35:23,955 --> 00:35:25,456
-I am.
-Nice to meet you.

427
00:35:25,523 --> 00:35:26,557
The pleasure is mine.

428
00:35:27,658 --> 00:35:29,360
<i>Seems we're in a similar business, John.</i>

429
00:35:29,827 --> 00:35:31,696
<i>-Really? How's that?</i>
<i>-Well, you're trying to...</i>

430
00:35:31,762 --> 00:35:33,464
predict people's behavior. So am I.

431
00:35:35,099 --> 00:35:37,068
When people come to us seeking coverage,

432
00:35:37,602 --> 00:35:40,838
we must analyze each person's likelihood of
success.

433
00:35:41,906 --> 00:35:43,941
-How do you do that?
-Well, it's a formula.

434
00:35:44,709 --> 00:35:47,478
It's a bit complicated, but basically,

435
00:35:47,545 --> 00:35:50,381
it's monthly payments multiplied by life
expectancy,

436
00:35:50,648 --> 00:35:52,216
minus the likelihood of illness,

437
00:35:52,483 --> 00:35:54,952
and if the result is positive, we cover them.

438
00:35:56,921 --> 00:36:00,458
-Who came up with that formula?
-I did.

439
00:36:01,425 --> 00:36:06,063
So, in a sense, you decide who lives or dies?

440
00:36:07,532 --> 00:36:09,033
No, I...

441
00:36:09,467 --> 00:36:11,469
No, I decide which people have the
potential...

442
00:36:11,736 --> 00:36:13,671
to live long, healthy lives.

443
00:36:14,605 --> 00:36:16,374
But you're not taking into account...

444
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:18,342
the most important human element of all.

445
00:36:18,709 --> 00:36:20,177
And what would that be?

446
00:36:20,244 --> 00:36:21,679
The will to live.

447
00:36:22,847 --> 00:36:25,016
Until someone faces death,

448
00:36:26,284 --> 00:36:28,185
you can't know whether they have what it
takes...

449
00:36:28,452 --> 00:36:30,054
to survive.

450
00:37:16,968 --> 00:37:18,736
TAKE THEM

451
00:37:22,873 --> 00:37:24,542
To hell with this!

452
00:37:27,378 --> 00:37:28,946
Shit!

453
00:37:53,137 --> 00:37:54,505
Hello, William.

454
00:37:54,772 --> 00:37:58,743
On the platforms behind me are two of your
colleagues.

455
00:37:59,310 --> 00:38:01,212
One is your file clerk.

456
00:38:01,479 --> 00:38:04,081
A healthy man with no living relatives.

457
00:38:04,882 --> 00:38:07,785
The other is a middle-aged woman

458
00:38:08,319 --> 00:38:10,688
with a family history of diabetes.

459
00:38:11,355 --> 00:38:12,990
According to your formula,

460
00:38:13,257 --> 00:38:16,060
your secretary is older and weaker,

461
00:38:16,327 --> 00:38:19,196
and therefore less worthy of living.

462
00:38:19,897 --> 00:38:23,200
But you know she'll be deeply missed by her
family,

463
00:38:23,267 --> 00:38:27,705
while young Allen would disappear without a
trace.

464
00:38:29,507 --> 00:38:31,709
Only one can leave this room,

465
00:38:31,776 --> 00:38:34,745
and the decision of who lives is yours.

466
00:38:35,846 --> 00:38:39,583
You must let one go to save the other.

467
00:38:39,850 --> 00:38:42,353
As you can see, the choice isn't so clear...

468
00:38:42,420 --> 00:38:44,055
when you're face-to-face with the people...

469
00:38:44,321 --> 00:38:46,590
whose blood will be on your hands.

470
00:38:48,159 --> 00:38:49,694
Let the game begin.

471
00:38:52,730 --> 00:38:55,499
Wait! No! Wait!

472
00:38:55,566 --> 00:38:57,668
I won't do it! I won't do it!

473
00:39:03,007 --> 00:39:03,941
Help me!

474
00:39:08,946 --> 00:39:10,347
It's a business!

475
00:39:10,881 --> 00:39:12,583
I don't make decisions like this!

476
00:39:16,187 --> 00:39:19,690
I won't do it! I won't do it!

477
00:39:20,624 --> 00:39:21,692
Stop!

478
00:39:25,896 --> 00:39:27,398
Help me, please.

479
00:40:01,432 --> 00:40:02,700
I'm sorry.

480
00:40:38,335 --> 00:40:39,570
Thank you.

481
00:40:40,738 --> 00:40:42,273
Thank you!

482
00:40:47,111 --> 00:40:49,580
Try to find a way out, okay?

483
00:40:49,647 --> 00:40:51,916
I have to keep going. Be careful.

484
00:41:22,847 --> 00:41:24,448
Why don't we pour acid on the bars?

485
00:41:24,515 --> 00:41:25,749
It'll eat through them, right?

486
00:41:26,083 --> 00:41:27,551
And what are we supposed to pour it with?

487
00:41:28,485 --> 00:41:29,787
Our hands?

488
00:41:33,724 --> 00:41:35,159
What are you looking at?

489
00:41:35,759 --> 00:41:38,629
I wonder if... The mirror.

490
00:41:40,130 --> 00:41:41,532
What?

491
00:41:42,199 --> 00:41:43,901
Who's behind the mirror?

492
00:41:46,503 --> 00:41:47,972
What do you want?

493
00:41:50,741 --> 00:41:52,610
Why are you doing this?

494
00:41:54,879 --> 00:41:58,515
Somebody help me, please. Please help me.

495
00:42:14,698 --> 00:42:16,100
<i>Hello, Pamela.</i>

496
00:42:16,166 --> 00:42:19,169
<i>You sensationalized my life, distorting the</i>
<i>truth...</i>

497
00:42:19,236 --> 00:42:22,072
<i>and exploiting my message for your own</i>
<i>benefit.</i>

498
00:42:48,565 --> 00:42:49,667
Hoffman.

499
00:42:49,733 --> 00:42:53,137
<i>It's Erickson. We found the Seth Baxter</i>
<i>recording.</i>

500
00:42:53,771 --> 00:42:55,005
Yeah?

501
00:42:55,306 --> 00:42:57,675
But there's something else we need to discuss
with you.

502
00:42:58,275 --> 00:43:00,644
-What is it?
-Better to do it in person.

503
00:43:01,178 --> 00:43:04,848
-We don't have much time.
-I'm on my way.

504
00:43:53,364 --> 00:43:57,167
<i>The device Timothy is in is my personal</i>
<i>favorite.</i>

505
00:43:57,434 --> 00:43:58,702
<i>I call it "The Rack."</i>

506
00:44:08,645 --> 00:44:12,216
Last-minute adjustments? I told you you'd mess
it up.

507
00:44:12,783 --> 00:44:14,284
If you change the gear ratio,

508
00:44:14,551 --> 00:44:16,120
you'll need to change the grease...

509
00:44:16,186 --> 00:44:18,122
to handle much more friction.

510
00:44:19,890 --> 00:44:21,925
Maybe you should stick to lifting heavy
things.

511
00:44:24,128 --> 00:44:27,331
You'll need a higher viscosity. It's in the
applicator.

512
00:44:29,800 --> 00:44:31,435
Next time, ask me.

513
00:44:32,069 --> 00:44:35,839
-How many next times will there be?
-As many as it takes.

514
00:44:46,083 --> 00:44:47,418
He's a human being.

515
00:44:51,722 --> 00:44:54,258
Do you enjoy the feeling of brutality, Mark?

516
00:44:54,525 --> 00:44:55,659
Let's be honest.

517
00:44:56,427 --> 00:44:58,429
We both want him to suffer.

518
00:45:22,786 --> 00:45:25,622
-And when is your test?
-I don't need one.

519
00:45:26,790 --> 00:45:28,392
-Really?
-Yes.

520
00:45:29,326 --> 00:45:33,263
Because I don't take my life for granted.

521
00:45:33,697 --> 00:45:36,266
You're still dragging your knuckles on the
ground like an ape.

522
00:45:37,201 --> 00:45:38,936
What do you know about life?

523
00:45:43,240 --> 00:45:46,443
Get used to me, because I'm not going
anywhere.

524
00:45:49,813 --> 00:45:51,548
Are you sure?

525
00:45:54,184 --> 00:45:55,552
There isn't much time.

526
00:46:01,458 --> 00:46:02,993
Are you okay?

527
00:46:06,296 --> 00:46:08,799
It's time to bring Dr. Denlon from the
hospital.

528
00:46:09,266 --> 00:46:10,200
All right.

529
00:46:11,635 --> 00:46:13,003
I'll take you back.

530
00:46:39,496 --> 00:46:41,165
Go. Hurry.

531
00:46:52,009 --> 00:46:54,511
-Why did you come here?
-John.

532
00:46:55,512 --> 00:46:57,014
Please, stop.

533
00:46:57,881 --> 00:46:59,850
-Don't do it.
-I promise...

534
00:47:02,886 --> 00:47:04,955
that when all this is over,

535
00:47:08,058 --> 00:47:10,227
I'll have a way out for you.

536
00:47:13,197 --> 00:47:14,865
I wish that moment were now.

537
00:47:16,533 --> 00:47:18,268
I have something for you.

538
00:47:32,649 --> 00:47:34,117
What's it for?

539
00:47:37,487 --> 00:47:39,056
When the time comes,

540
00:47:41,124 --> 00:47:42,559
you'll know what to do.

541
00:48:26,603 --> 00:48:27,604
FINAL DECISION

542
00:48:27,671 --> 00:48:29,606
<i>Will, I came to talk to you...</i>

543
00:48:32,442 --> 00:48:34,278
because I found a treatment for my cancer...

544
00:48:34,344 --> 00:48:35,979
that I think has potential,

545
00:48:36,413 --> 00:48:41,918
but my requests for coverage were denied.

546
00:48:43,086 --> 00:48:45,822
So I was hoping that if I came and explained
it to you,

547
00:48:46,356 --> 00:48:48,392
you might reverse the denial.

548
00:48:52,029 --> 00:48:54,398
Well, let's hear what you have to say, John.
I'm listening.

549
00:48:58,769 --> 00:49:00,537
There's a doctor in Norway.

550
00:49:00,804 --> 00:49:03,940
He has a success rate of 30 to 40 percent...

551
00:49:04,007 --> 00:49:05,309
with a form of gene therapy.

552
00:49:05,575 --> 00:49:08,045
He injects what he calls suicide genes...

553
00:49:08,378 --> 00:49:10,247
into cancer cells.

554
00:49:10,781 --> 00:49:14,484
Then he administers an inactive form of a
toxic drug...

555
00:49:14,551 --> 00:49:17,120
Yes, I know the therapy you're talking about.

556
00:49:17,721 --> 00:49:20,190
All right. And they're about to start new
trials.

557
00:49:20,624 --> 00:49:22,125
He's looking for new patients,

558
00:49:22,526 --> 00:49:24,661
and he thinks I'm the perfect candidate.

559
00:49:24,728 --> 00:49:26,930
John, if your attending physician, Dr.
Gordon...

560
00:49:26,997 --> 00:49:29,566
had thought you were a good candidate, he
would have tried it.

561
00:49:31,201 --> 00:49:33,003
Dr. Gordon is a specialist.

562
00:49:33,070 --> 00:49:34,871
He makes money from his specialty.

563
00:49:34,938 --> 00:49:36,073
He's not a thinker.

564
00:49:36,340 --> 00:49:39,309
I mean, every time I go there he's looking at
the clock.

565
00:49:39,376 --> 00:49:40,744
I'll be direct.

566
00:49:40,811 --> 00:49:42,879
At your age, with your cancer so advanced...

567
00:49:43,146 --> 00:49:45,115
it simply isn't feasible for us...

568
00:49:45,182 --> 00:49:47,017
Wait, wait. What isn't feasible?

569
00:49:47,884 --> 00:49:51,655
According to whose mathematical equation isn't
it feasible?

570
00:49:51,922 --> 00:49:53,657
It's policy, John.

571
00:49:54,157 --> 00:49:55,459
It's policy.

572
00:49:56,226 --> 00:49:58,995
And if you go ahead with this treatment
anyway,

573
00:49:59,062 --> 00:50:02,766
which we consider ineffective, you'll breach
your contract...

574
00:50:03,033 --> 00:50:05,235
and lose your coverage altogether.

575
00:50:06,436 --> 00:50:07,904
I'm sorry.

576
00:50:19,149 --> 00:50:21,818
Did you know that in the East, people pay...

577
00:50:22,085 --> 00:50:23,820
their doctors when they're healthy?

578
00:50:24,588 --> 00:50:26,456
When they get sick, they don't have to pay.

579
00:50:28,658 --> 00:50:31,361
So basically, they end up paying for what they
want,

580
00:50:32,496 --> 00:50:34,498
not something they don't want.

581
00:50:35,966 --> 00:50:38,001
Here, we have it backward.

582
00:50:40,470 --> 00:50:44,508
Politicians keep saying the same thing over
and over.

583
00:50:44,808 --> 00:50:46,476
Decisions about healthcare...

584
00:50:46,777 --> 00:50:48,311
should be made by doctors and their patients,

585
00:50:49,012 --> 00:50:50,147
not by governments.

586
00:50:50,414 --> 00:50:53,283
I know it's not doctors, patients,

587
00:50:53,850 --> 00:50:55,419
or the government making those decisions.

588
00:50:57,287 --> 00:50:59,423
It's the goddamn insurance companies.

589
00:51:01,625 --> 00:51:03,160
Piranhas.

590
00:51:04,528 --> 00:51:06,062
John,

591
00:51:07,464 --> 00:51:08,565
please.

592
00:51:08,832 --> 00:51:10,467
If you do this, you're on your own,

593
00:51:10,534 --> 00:51:12,803
and the costs you'll face will be staggering.

594
00:51:12,869 --> 00:51:15,772
Don't talk to me about money. I have money.

595
00:51:17,741 --> 00:51:19,342
This is a matter of principle.

596
00:51:23,647 --> 00:51:28,051
You see, Will, we're talking about my life.

597
00:51:28,485 --> 00:51:30,520
-Remember?
-And what about Jill's life?

598
00:51:30,587 --> 00:51:31,988
Who's going to take care of her after you're
gone?

599
00:51:32,255 --> 00:51:33,757
Let me worry about Jill.

600
00:51:34,558 --> 00:51:38,562
The kind of cancer you have is malignant and
inoperable.

601
00:51:38,628 --> 00:51:40,497
You put that very delicately.

602
00:51:40,564 --> 00:51:42,332
Even if the treatment works,

603
00:51:42,599 --> 00:51:43,733
the cancer will come back sooner or later.

604
00:51:43,800 --> 00:51:44,801
It's a battle you can't win.

605
00:51:44,868 --> 00:51:46,002
That was even more delicate.

606
00:51:47,137 --> 00:51:49,039
In fact, you're very clever.

607
00:51:51,708 --> 00:51:54,377
I think it's the living who will have...

608
00:51:54,644 --> 00:51:56,112
the final judgment over you,

609
00:51:56,179 --> 00:51:59,549
because the dead have no claim on your soul.

610
00:52:10,093 --> 00:52:12,162
But maybe you're wrong.

611
00:52:27,577 --> 00:52:28,745
Shit.

612
00:52:37,287 --> 00:52:38,522
<i>Hello, William.</i>

613
00:52:38,788 --> 00:52:41,091
<i>You've seen the flaws in your formula.</i>

614
00:52:41,525 --> 00:52:44,327
<i>But what you haven't seen is how far people</i>
<i>will go...</i>

615
00:52:44,895 --> 00:52:46,596
<i>when they're faced with death.</i>

616
00:52:51,101 --> 00:52:53,904
<i>Your company's attorney...</i>

617
00:52:54,170 --> 00:52:56,172
<i>has 90 seconds to cross this room,</i>

618
00:52:56,239 --> 00:52:57,841
<i>or the device attached to her chest...</i>

619
00:52:58,108 --> 00:53:00,510
<i>will activate and pierce her brain.</i>

620
00:53:00,777 --> 00:53:02,812
Everything's going to be all right! You'll be
okay!

621
00:53:03,313 --> 00:53:05,782
<i>She'll discover that her journey through the</i>
<i>room...</i>

622
00:53:06,082 --> 00:53:07,584
<i>is filled with danger.</i>

623
00:53:09,486 --> 00:53:13,757
<i>In order to succeed, you'll have to help</i>
<i>her...</i>

624
00:53:14,157 --> 00:53:18,628
<i>for you hold the key to her survival.</i>

625
00:53:20,530 --> 00:53:24,000
<i>When faced with death, will she have what it</i>
<i>takes to live?</i>

626
00:53:24,734 --> 00:53:26,202
<i>Let the game begin.</i>

627
00:53:30,240 --> 00:53:31,174
All right, listen!

628
00:53:32,242 --> 00:53:33,343
What the hell is this?

629
00:53:33,410 --> 00:53:36,012
Listen to me! Debbie!

630
00:53:38,148 --> 00:53:39,616
Get me out of here!

631
00:53:43,954 --> 00:53:46,756
Get through the maze as quickly as you can.

632
00:53:46,823 --> 00:53:48,191
Go ahead. Move! Now!

633
00:53:51,361 --> 00:53:52,762
Come on! You can do it!

634
00:53:53,263 --> 00:53:55,231
You can do it! You have to keep moving!

635
00:53:56,066 --> 00:53:57,500
Will, this is crazy.

636
00:53:57,901 --> 00:54:02,238
Keep going! You can make it! You have to keep
moving!

637
00:54:07,644 --> 00:54:08,745
RELIEVE HER

638
00:54:08,812 --> 00:54:10,213
Wait a minute. I think I can help you.

639
00:54:10,480 --> 00:54:12,382
-It's too hot!
-Maybe this will stop it.

640
00:54:15,251 --> 00:54:17,120
Yes! Yes!

641
00:54:17,854 --> 00:54:19,222
Do it again! It worked!

642
00:54:20,390 --> 00:54:21,825
Do it again! It worked!

643
00:54:22,325 --> 00:54:24,094
-Ready?
-Yes!

644
00:54:24,628 --> 00:54:25,895
Go!

645
00:54:39,576 --> 00:54:43,213
Keep going, don't stop!

646
00:54:48,885 --> 00:54:50,954
Debbie, keep moving.

647
00:54:52,889 --> 00:54:55,725
Come on! Go! Go! Go!

648
00:54:58,461 --> 00:55:00,630
I can't! It's a goddamn trap!

649
00:55:00,697 --> 00:55:01,898
There's no way out.

650
00:55:03,967 --> 00:55:05,702
It's a goddamn trap. I can't get out!

651
00:55:08,672 --> 00:55:11,408
No! No, there's a ladder at the end!

652
00:55:11,808 --> 00:55:13,643
If you get there, I can help you.

653
00:55:16,546 --> 00:55:18,081
Ready?

654
00:55:20,050 --> 00:55:21,351
Go!

655
00:55:40,003 --> 00:55:40,937
Come on!

656
00:55:41,838 --> 00:55:46,676
Come on! Come on! Climb! Come on!

657
00:55:46,743 --> 00:55:49,145
You're almost there.

658
00:55:51,548 --> 00:55:52,816
Come on! Come on! Come on!

659
00:55:54,150 --> 00:55:56,419
Find a key. There has to be a key.

660
00:55:57,120 --> 00:55:58,555
Find a key!

661
00:56:01,391 --> 00:56:03,893
What? What?

662
00:56:04,494 --> 00:56:07,130
-What are you waiting for?
-It's inside you.

663
00:56:08,331 --> 00:56:09,966
-What?
-It's inside you!

664
00:56:10,600 --> 00:56:12,102
Shit.

665
00:56:13,570 --> 00:56:16,740
Okay. I can do this.

666
00:56:18,074 --> 00:56:20,810
Wait! What are you doing?

667
00:56:20,877 --> 00:56:22,178
I need that fucking key.

668
00:56:23,012 --> 00:56:23,947
Wait! No!

669
00:56:42,599 --> 00:56:44,267
I can get it out! I can get it out!

670
00:56:47,904 --> 00:56:49,572
For God's sake, no!

671
00:57:17,901 --> 00:57:19,302
<i>Hello, Pamela.</i>

672
00:57:21,337 --> 00:57:25,542
<i>Today you'll experience the meaning of</i>
<i>sacrifice,</i>

673
00:57:25,608 --> 00:57:27,443
<i>and witness the consequences...</i>

674
00:57:27,710 --> 00:57:29,913
<i>for those who have unjustly harmed others.</i>

675
00:57:30,313 --> 00:57:31,247
Go to hell!

676
00:57:34,184 --> 00:57:35,251
WATCH IT WORK

677
00:57:52,735 --> 00:57:54,137
There's someone there.

678
00:57:55,605 --> 00:57:57,073
They're watching us.

679
00:57:59,709 --> 00:58:01,144
Why?

680
00:58:01,411 --> 00:58:03,079
Why would someone do this?

681
00:58:05,648 --> 00:58:06,749
LIVE OR DIE

682
00:58:12,956 --> 00:58:14,190
Thanks for coming.

683
00:58:14,791 --> 00:58:15,825
What did you find?

684
00:58:15,892 --> 00:58:17,660
An anomaly in Strahm's fingerprints.

685
00:58:17,727 --> 00:58:18,895
-What?
-Look.

686
00:58:21,764 --> 00:58:24,133
You see, human fingerprints leave behind an
oily residue.

687
00:58:24,200 --> 00:58:26,369
And depending on how long they've been
exposed,

688
00:58:26,436 --> 00:58:28,271
they're extremely susceptible to
contamination.

689
00:58:28,538 --> 00:58:29,572
So what's the problem?

690
00:58:29,639 --> 00:58:31,741
Well, we found traces of halomethane R-12.

691
00:58:32,775 --> 00:58:34,244
You mean Freon gas.

692
00:58:34,844 --> 00:58:37,347
Production of R-12 was discontinued in 1994,

693
00:58:37,413 --> 00:58:38,615
so the question is:

694
00:58:38,681 --> 00:58:40,416
Was the contaminant we found already there?

695
00:58:41,050 --> 00:58:43,286
Or did Strahm bring it into the building?

696
00:58:43,353 --> 00:58:44,854
We've been looking into what was in the
building...

697
00:58:44,921 --> 00:58:46,256
before it was abandoned.

698
00:58:48,324 --> 00:58:49,459
Excuse me.

699
00:58:50,727 --> 00:58:52,862
Did you find anything on the Seth Baxter
recording?

700
00:58:53,162 --> 00:58:56,165
Actually, yes. We may be onto something.

701
00:58:56,766 --> 00:58:58,101
The tape was in very bad shape...

702
00:58:58,167 --> 00:59:00,169
when we found it, but we managed to
authenticate it.

703
00:59:00,436 --> 00:59:02,405
The voice was deliberately distorted,

704
00:59:02,472 --> 00:59:04,040
but it doesn't match John Kramer's.

705
00:59:04,807 --> 00:59:06,109
They're analyzing it now.

706
00:59:07,176 --> 00:59:08,278
What for?

707
00:59:08,344 --> 00:59:11,247
We're trying to decode the tape to recover the
original voice.

708
00:59:12,215 --> 00:59:13,850
That's what the technicians are working on.

709
00:59:17,086 --> 00:59:19,789
Are you timing something? Your watch.

710
00:59:21,257 --> 00:59:23,726
I was before I got your call.

711
00:59:25,328 --> 00:59:28,464
We've got it. It's the smoking gun.

712
00:59:29,899 --> 00:59:31,200
The lab isn't here.

713
00:59:31,801 --> 00:59:34,304
Come on. You're coming, right?

714
00:59:36,406 --> 00:59:37,874
Of course.

715
01:00:14,277 --> 01:00:16,179
<i>You always try to control every situation.</i>

716
01:00:16,245 --> 01:00:20,183
<i>-I don't know what you're talking about.</i>
<i>-Go to hell!</i>

717
01:00:20,450 --> 01:00:23,586
<i>Can you finish it? Let's see what we can do.</i>

718
01:00:23,653 --> 01:00:25,555
Hey! Who's there?

719
01:00:25,989 --> 01:00:28,191
-Mr. Easton, is that you?
-Yes. Shelby?

720
01:00:28,658 --> 01:00:29,892
Who's there with you?

721
01:00:30,526 --> 01:00:32,328
<i>We're all here, sir. All six of us.</i>

722
01:00:32,395 --> 01:00:33,396
<i>What the hell is happening?</i>

723
01:00:34,831 --> 01:00:36,199
What can you see?

724
01:00:36,599 --> 01:00:38,301
<i>Nothing. It's dark.</i>

725
01:00:39,902 --> 01:00:41,237
What's that sound?

726
01:00:41,504 --> 01:00:42,972
<i>Something we're chained to.</i>

727
01:00:44,741 --> 01:00:45,842
<i>Get us out of here!</i>

728
01:00:45,908 --> 01:00:48,277
<i>Please help us! You have to help us!</i>

729
01:00:49,612 --> 01:00:51,447
<i>Get us out of here, Mr. E!</i>

730
01:00:59,222 --> 01:01:02,058
Mr. Easton! Mr. Easton!

731
01:01:03,026 --> 01:01:04,394
You have to get us out of here!

732
01:01:07,196 --> 01:01:09,265
Get us out! Come on!

733
01:01:11,801 --> 01:01:13,369
-Please.
-Please.

734
01:01:13,970 --> 01:01:14,971
Please get us out of here!

735
01:01:16,105 --> 01:01:17,740
Mr. Easton, what is this?

736
01:01:20,243 --> 01:01:21,477
It's a game.

737
01:01:22,311 --> 01:01:24,514
A game? What are you talking about?

738
01:01:34,090 --> 01:01:35,558
<i>Hello, William.</i>

739
01:01:35,625 --> 01:01:39,128
<i>Before you are six of your most valuable</i>
<i>associates.</i>

740
01:01:39,429 --> 01:01:42,031
<i>The ones who find errors in policies.</i>

741
01:01:42,398 --> 01:01:45,802
<i>Their findings result in denying or</i>
<i>terminating...</i>

742
01:01:45,868 --> 01:01:48,671
<i>two-thirds of all applications.</i>

743
01:01:49,272 --> 01:01:51,941
<i>Now you must apply your formula to them.</i>

744
01:01:52,575 --> 01:01:54,811
<i>Will you be able to find their errors?</i>

745
01:01:56,012 --> 01:01:57,980
<i>Six ride the carousel,</i>

746
01:02:01,484 --> 01:02:03,853
<i>but only two can get off.</i>

747
01:02:04,454 --> 01:02:07,857
<i>The decision of which two survive is yours.</i>

748
01:02:08,458 --> 01:02:11,260
<i>But remember: the loaded gun will continue...</i>

749
01:02:11,327 --> 01:02:14,464
<i>firing until all six shots are spent.</i>

750
01:02:15,531 --> 01:02:19,302
<i>If you make no decision, all six will die.</i>

751
01:02:21,537 --> 01:02:23,339
<i>To save the two you've chosen,</i>

752
01:02:23,606 --> 01:02:26,175
<i>you must press both buttons at once...</i>

753
01:02:26,442 --> 01:02:28,144
<i>on the box in front of you.</i>

754
01:02:28,411 --> 01:02:32,148
<i>However, doing so requires a sacrifice.</i>

755
01:02:32,715 --> 01:02:34,884
<i>Two can live. Four will die.</i>

756
01:02:35,485 --> 01:02:39,555
<i>Your decision is symbolized by the blood on</i>
<i>your hands.</i>

757
01:02:45,194 --> 01:02:47,763
Wake up! You have to get us out of here!

758
01:02:51,000 --> 01:02:54,337
Listen to me! I'm the one who deserves to
live.

759
01:02:55,605 --> 01:02:56,539
Shit!

760
01:03:00,643 --> 01:03:02,044
Oh, God!

761
01:03:03,112 --> 01:03:06,816
Please, Mr. Easton!

762
01:03:06,883 --> 01:03:10,920
Follow the policy, Mr. Easton! Please do it!

763
01:03:11,220 --> 01:03:12,855
Follow the policy, Mr. Easton!

764
01:03:13,523 --> 01:03:14,690
Do it!

765
01:03:19,328 --> 01:03:20,263
Shit!

766
01:03:23,032 --> 01:03:24,066
Oh, God!

767
01:03:29,105 --> 01:03:30,039
Fuck it!

768
01:03:30,706 --> 01:03:32,542
Mr. E! You always said...

769
01:03:32,808 --> 01:03:34,076
we're only as strong as our weakest link.

770
01:03:34,143 --> 01:03:35,945
I'm strong! You can't let me die!

771
01:03:36,212 --> 01:03:37,246
Fuck you!

772
01:03:37,313 --> 01:03:38,981
I'm healthy! I'm healthy!

773
01:03:42,084 --> 01:03:43,286
I have two kids!

774
01:03:43,653 --> 01:03:44,820
You know them, Will!

775
01:03:45,588 --> 01:03:47,456
Please, they can't live without me!

776
01:03:47,857 --> 01:03:49,091
They need me!

777
01:04:11,280 --> 01:04:13,416
Thank you, thank you.

778
01:04:16,419 --> 01:04:17,787
You don't even know her, Mr. Easton!

779
01:04:20,423 --> 01:04:21,557
My parents are sick!

780
01:04:21,624 --> 01:04:23,025
They need me! I'm all they have!

781
01:04:23,292 --> 01:04:27,964
-Bullshit! Your parents hate you!
-What? That's not true.

782
01:04:28,030 --> 01:04:29,899
Yes, it is! I sit next to you.

783
01:04:30,299 --> 01:04:34,403
-I'm pregnant!
-No, she's not! She's lying!

784
01:04:35,671 --> 01:04:36,906
It's true!

785
01:04:37,640 --> 01:04:38,774
She's lying!

786
01:04:38,841 --> 01:04:39,809
No, no!

787
01:04:39,875 --> 01:04:41,477
-I'm pregnant!
-She's lying!

788
01:04:44,480 --> 01:04:48,351
Please! Shit! Press that thing!

789
01:04:48,417 --> 01:04:49,719
She's lying!

790
01:04:50,786 --> 01:04:52,154
Mr. Easton, press it!

791
01:04:56,459 --> 01:04:57,393
Press it!

792
01:05:06,836 --> 01:05:09,038
Trust me, I wouldn't lie to you.

793
01:05:09,105 --> 01:05:10,573
He's a kiss-ass!

794
01:05:10,640 --> 01:05:12,341
Mr. Easton! My parents are rich!

795
01:05:12,642 --> 01:05:14,644
They'll give you whatever you want! Just name
it!

796
01:05:14,910 --> 01:05:17,346
No, he's a thief! He's been stealing from the
company for years!

797
01:05:17,413 --> 01:05:19,015
-Don't believe him!
-It's true!

798
01:05:21,183 --> 01:05:22,818
-Dave!
-Fuck you!

799
01:05:23,085 --> 01:05:24,120
It's true!

800
01:05:24,186 --> 01:05:26,789
Don't listen to them, Mr. E!

801
01:05:30,559 --> 01:05:31,627
Come on, Mr. Easton!

802
01:05:40,703 --> 01:05:42,438
Mr. Easton, listen to me carefully!

803
01:05:42,505 --> 01:05:45,207
I'm on your side. And I can help you.

804
01:05:46,575 --> 01:05:48,611
But you can't trust this bitch!

805
01:05:48,678 --> 01:05:49,812
What?

806
01:05:51,380 --> 01:05:53,215
-She's a liar!
-What?

807
01:05:53,549 --> 01:05:56,085
You can't believe her. She's already trying to
lie.

808
01:05:56,852 --> 01:05:58,688
And she'll keep doing it!

809
01:06:03,459 --> 01:06:05,361
And what about you? He wants your job.

810
01:06:05,728 --> 01:06:09,632
He'd cut your throat at the first chance.

811
01:06:10,533 --> 01:06:11,734
Please!

812
01:06:12,902 --> 01:06:14,337
Save me! Save me!

813
01:06:15,371 --> 01:06:16,706
No, please!

814
01:06:24,680 --> 01:06:26,082
Shit!

815
01:06:37,259 --> 01:06:38,994
Oh, God! Thank you!

816
01:06:40,429 --> 01:06:41,630
Thank God.

817
01:06:42,965 --> 01:06:45,801
That's it, isn't it? It's over!

818
01:06:46,369 --> 01:06:48,404
You motherfucker!

819
01:06:49,572 --> 01:06:53,509
You spineless motherfucker!

820
01:06:55,010 --> 01:06:56,579
That's all it takes!

821
01:06:56,846 --> 01:06:59,315
One bitch says something and it's all over!

822
01:06:59,982 --> 01:07:04,487
You know what, William? Your formula is
bullshit!

823
01:07:05,588 --> 01:07:07,590
Bullshit!

824
01:07:08,391 --> 01:07:10,393
Listen to me, you son of a bitch!

825
01:07:11,694 --> 01:07:13,362
I did everything for you.

826
01:07:19,068 --> 01:07:22,705
Look at me! Look at me while you kill me!

827
01:07:38,921 --> 01:07:40,856
Oh, God!

828
01:07:41,991 --> 01:07:43,225
Thank you.

829
01:07:57,973 --> 01:08:00,676
AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY

830
01:08:11,253 --> 01:08:13,389
<i>Right now, you're feeling helpless.</i>

831
01:08:15,090 --> 01:08:17,293
<i>Right now, you're feeling helpless.</i>

832
01:08:19,195 --> 01:08:21,263
<i>Right now, you're feeling helpless.</i>

833
01:08:23,833 --> 01:08:25,167
How's it going?

834
01:08:25,734 --> 01:08:26,669
Good.

835
01:08:28,170 --> 01:08:29,538
Here it is.

836
01:08:30,406 --> 01:08:31,340
Whoever made this tape...

837
01:08:31,640 --> 01:08:33,843
had access to very sophisticated audio
equipment.

838
01:08:34,944 --> 01:08:36,612
<i>Right now, you're feeling helpless.</i>

839
01:08:37,446 --> 01:08:40,082
Luckily, we've got a few tricks of our own.

840
01:08:40,483 --> 01:08:43,919
I can reverse the algorithm and hear the real
voice.

841
01:08:47,823 --> 01:08:49,024
Let's hear what you've got.

842
01:08:56,332 --> 01:08:57,500
You okay?

843
01:08:59,201 --> 01:09:01,003
You look a little worried.

844
01:09:01,704 --> 01:09:03,172
I'm anxious to hear the recording.

845
01:09:11,881 --> 01:09:15,384
There are still some things that don't add up.

846
01:09:17,019 --> 01:09:19,455
I don't understand Strahm's motivation.

847
01:09:20,990 --> 01:09:22,758
He was my partner for five years.

848
01:09:23,025 --> 01:09:24,927
I mean, we all knew he had a temper,

849
01:09:25,261 --> 01:09:27,763
but I never saw any indication of psychotic
behavior.

850
01:09:28,564 --> 01:09:31,300
You never know when someone's rotten on the
inside.

851
01:09:36,138 --> 01:09:37,306
<i>...helpless.</i>

852
01:09:38,007 --> 01:09:39,108
Do you hear that?

853
01:09:40,876 --> 01:09:42,211
I'm getting there.

854
01:09:42,578 --> 01:09:44,213
Come on, I thought you had something to show
us.

855
01:09:44,880 --> 01:09:48,050
It's just that I never thought of him as
vindictive.

856
01:09:50,319 --> 01:09:52,021
All the facts are there.

857
01:09:56,825 --> 01:09:58,627
But something isn't right.

858
01:09:58,694 --> 01:10:00,429
Listen, I'm getting closer.

859
01:10:01,730 --> 01:10:03,265
You know, there's another possibility.

860
01:10:03,899 --> 01:10:07,603
Let's say Strahm killed Seth Baxter
specifically...

861
01:10:07,670 --> 01:10:09,772
to make you look like Jigsaw's accomplice.

862
01:10:10,940 --> 01:10:12,107
All right.

863
01:10:13,475 --> 01:10:14,810
But there's a problem.

864
01:10:15,878 --> 01:10:18,047
Further analysis of Strahm's fingerprints...

865
01:10:18,113 --> 01:10:21,417
revealed that the uric acid levels...

866
01:10:21,784 --> 01:10:23,152
were inconsistent...

867
01:10:23,218 --> 01:10:26,555
with someone whose metabolism was still
active.

868
01:10:27,256 --> 01:10:28,390
In other words?

869
01:10:28,891 --> 01:10:29,925
In other words,

870
01:10:29,992 --> 01:10:31,794
when you planted his fingerprints on the
latest victim,

871
01:10:31,860 --> 01:10:33,395
Strahm was already dead!

872
01:10:34,263 --> 01:10:35,497
There it is.

873
01:10:36,098 --> 01:10:38,067
<i>Right now, you're feeling helpless.</i>

874
01:11:03,125 --> 01:11:04,593
Who else knows?

875
01:11:07,529 --> 01:11:08,964
Who knows about me?

876
01:11:10,799 --> 01:11:12,301
Everyone!

877
01:11:12,568 --> 01:11:13,736
You're lying.

878
01:11:14,370 --> 01:11:15,671
You're a goddamn liar.

879
01:14:02,271 --> 01:14:03,572
I found something you'll want to see.

880
01:14:03,872 --> 01:14:05,440
It was at the place where John died.

881
01:14:05,874 --> 01:14:07,176
Does it mean anything to you?

882
01:14:12,781 --> 01:14:13,782
What are you doing?

883
01:14:13,849 --> 01:14:16,018
Get as far away from the sprinklers as you
can.

884
01:14:16,084 --> 01:14:17,419
We don't know what they do.

885
01:14:17,486 --> 01:14:19,321
We're not just going to sit here doing
nothing.

886
01:14:19,388 --> 01:14:21,156
We have to make a decision, don't you
understand?

887
01:14:44,246 --> 01:14:46,682
Okay. You can pull it.

888
01:14:47,482 --> 01:14:48,717
I love you.

889
01:14:49,518 --> 01:14:50,686
I love you too.

890
01:14:52,087 --> 01:14:53,355
Get back.

891
01:14:56,458 --> 01:14:58,827
No. You don't know what it does.

892
01:15:09,938 --> 01:15:11,106
On the count of three.

893
01:15:14,076 --> 01:15:15,210
No. No.

894
01:15:17,813 --> 01:15:18,814
One,

895
01:15:20,349 --> 01:15:21,450
two,

896
01:15:23,385 --> 01:15:24,653
three!

897
01:15:26,788 --> 01:15:27,956
What the hell?

898
01:15:31,193 --> 01:15:32,461
It doesn't work!

899
01:15:33,195 --> 01:15:34,730
This fucking thing doesn't work!

900
01:15:35,130 --> 01:15:36,231
What the hell?

901
01:15:41,803 --> 01:15:43,338
I think I know what it does.

902
01:15:59,288 --> 01:16:02,424
Get used to me, because I'm not going
anywhere.

903
01:16:02,491 --> 01:16:03,992
Are you sure?

904
01:16:09,698 --> 01:16:10,966
Amanda will fail you.

905
01:16:12,901 --> 01:16:13,902
We'll see.

906
01:16:20,442 --> 01:16:22,644
<i>Amanda, you were with Cecil...</i>

907
01:16:22,711 --> 01:16:24,446
<i>the night Jill lost Gideon.</i>

908
01:16:28,750 --> 01:16:31,119
-What the hell are you doing? Go!
-I don't want to.

909
01:16:31,186 --> 01:16:32,120
What do you mean?

910
01:16:33,055 --> 01:16:35,724
Run, you coward! Go, go, go!

911
01:16:39,728 --> 01:16:40,662
<i>Listen to me.</i>

912
01:16:46,735 --> 01:16:48,136
<i>You killed her child.</i>

913
01:16:49,838 --> 01:16:51,406
<i>You know it, and I know it.</i>

914
01:16:51,840 --> 01:16:53,642
<i>So do exactly what I tell you.</i>

915
01:16:54,309 --> 01:16:55,577
<i>Kill Lynn Denlon...</i>

916
01:16:56,411 --> 01:16:58,847
<i>or I'll tell John what you did.</i>

917
01:17:00,248 --> 01:17:02,050
She doesn't deserve to be free.

918
01:17:02,317 --> 01:17:05,020
Amanda, Lynn is more important than you think.

919
01:17:54,102 --> 01:17:56,505
I did it. I did it.

920
01:17:57,739 --> 01:17:59,007
I did it!

921
01:17:59,708 --> 01:18:02,878
<i>If you fail, you'll never see your family</i>
<i>again.</i>

922
01:18:10,619 --> 01:18:12,421
-What are you doing here?
-Will!

923
01:18:13,989 --> 01:18:15,223
Pamela.

924
01:18:16,091 --> 01:18:17,092
Yeah.

925
01:18:17,159 --> 01:18:19,294
I know it's your birthday, and I'll make it up
to you.

926
01:18:19,361 --> 01:18:20,495
I promise.

927
01:18:20,562 --> 01:18:22,531
Are you really standing up your sister for
work?

928
01:18:22,864 --> 01:18:24,132
I'm your only family.

929
01:18:25,133 --> 01:18:26,234
Are you okay?

930
01:18:28,670 --> 01:18:29,838
Let her go!

931
01:18:29,905 --> 01:18:30,906
I did it! I won!

932
01:18:32,107 --> 01:18:34,376
<i>I followed your goddamn rules! Now let me go.</i>

933
01:18:35,310 --> 01:18:36,978
You bastard.

934
01:18:38,380 --> 01:18:39,915
<i>You just gave me a death sentence.</i>

935
01:18:40,182 --> 01:18:41,149
<i>Who's going to cover me now?</i>

936
01:18:41,850 --> 01:18:42,918
You just killed me.

937
01:18:51,993 --> 01:18:53,628
You're the one who killed my dad.

938
01:19:15,617 --> 01:19:16,952
<i>Hello, Tara.</i>

939
01:19:17,018 --> 01:19:20,589
<i>I apologize for treating you and your son this</i>
<i>way,</i>

940
01:19:21,223 --> 01:19:23,925
<i>but I assure you it's for a reason.</i>

941
01:19:24,793 --> 01:19:27,496
<i>The man before you has just made sacrifices...</i>

942
01:19:27,562 --> 01:19:30,098
<i>to save the lives of his loved ones.</i>

943
01:19:30,365 --> 01:19:31,967
<i>However, when he had the chance...</i>

944
01:19:32,033 --> 01:19:35,604
<i>to save your husband's life, he chose not to.</i>

945
01:19:36,471 --> 01:19:39,708
<i>Now you have the power to save a life.</i>

946
01:19:40,675 --> 01:19:41,843
<i>Will you give this man...</i>

947
01:19:42,110 --> 01:19:44,146
<i>the chance to go on living?</i>

948
01:19:44,513 --> 01:19:46,781
<i>Or will you give him the same death</i>
<i>sentence...</i>

949
01:19:47,048 --> 01:19:48,817
<i>that he gave your husband?</i>

950
01:19:49,117 --> 01:19:50,252
<i>Live or die.</i>

951
01:19:51,586 --> 01:19:52,854
<i>The choice is yours.</i>

952
01:19:59,628 --> 01:20:01,163
It's not my game.

953
01:20:08,270 --> 01:20:09,905
<i>I'm leaving you a box today,</i>

954
01:20:10,405 --> 01:20:13,608
<i>and its contents are very important.</i>

955
01:20:16,678 --> 01:20:18,146
Mrs. Tuck, what's in the box?

956
01:20:44,773 --> 01:20:45,941
Please.

957
01:20:47,042 --> 01:20:48,376
Please forgive me.

958
01:20:48,910 --> 01:20:50,579
Have mercy, please!

959
01:20:50,946 --> 01:20:54,249
Did he show mercy when my husband was
suffering?

960
01:21:05,493 --> 01:21:07,262
John left you five envelopes.

961
01:21:08,763 --> 01:21:10,732
The sixth was for me.

962
01:21:15,870 --> 01:21:17,472
This is John's will.

963
01:21:23,478 --> 01:21:26,514
-He didn't have a choice!
-He always had a choice!

964
01:21:28,883 --> 01:21:32,387
I'm doing this because I can't forgive him...

965
01:21:32,954 --> 01:21:34,322
for what he did to me.

966
01:21:36,625 --> 01:21:40,562
I'm doing this because I couldn't forgive
myself...

967
01:21:40,629 --> 01:21:43,298
for what I might do to someone else.

968
01:21:43,565 --> 01:21:44,532
Please, no.

969
01:21:46,635 --> 01:21:49,337
-He won't kill again.
-Please.

970
01:21:58,513 --> 01:22:00,115
Please.

971
01:22:01,149 --> 01:22:03,318
I can't. I can't kill him.

972
01:22:08,823 --> 01:22:10,191
I can.

973
01:22:11,026 --> 01:22:12,027
No, Brent!

974
01:22:12,093 --> 01:22:13,895
You killed my father, you son of a bitch!

975
01:22:17,666 --> 01:22:18,867
Now you'll burn in hell.

976
01:22:45,994 --> 01:22:46,995
Those are the rules, Harold.

977
01:22:47,262 --> 01:22:48,630
You just gave me a death sentence.

978
01:22:48,697 --> 01:22:51,833
In a sense, you decide who lives or dies?

979
01:22:54,869 --> 01:22:56,938
It's policy, John. It's policy.

980
01:22:57,305 --> 01:22:58,506
Who came up with that formula?

981
01:23:04,479 --> 01:23:05,747
I think it's the living...

982
01:23:06,314 --> 01:23:08,483
who will have the final judgment over you,

983
01:23:09,150 --> 01:23:11,720
because the dead have no claim on your soul.

984
01:23:16,324 --> 01:23:18,026
But maybe you're wrong.

985
01:23:24,332 --> 01:23:25,667
Game over.

986
01:23:26,935 --> 01:23:28,570
No! No!

987
01:23:55,530 --> 01:23:56,998
When will your test be, Detective?

988
01:23:59,901 --> 01:24:01,035
I don't need one.

989
01:24:01,369 --> 01:24:02,303
Really?

990
01:24:10,445 --> 01:24:11,846
You know why you're here, don't you?

991
01:24:17,352 --> 01:24:20,422
I want you to know what it takes to survive.

992
01:24:21,189 --> 01:24:23,858
You think you have everything under control
now, don't you?

993
01:24:23,925 --> 01:24:26,728
You think you can walk away without being
tested?

994
01:24:41,643 --> 01:24:43,778
I'm just carrying out John's final wishes.

995
01:24:44,379 --> 01:24:46,881
Do you enjoy the feeling of brutality, Mark?

996
01:24:48,383 --> 01:24:50,919
From now on, I control every aspect of the
game.

997
01:24:50,985 --> 01:24:52,187
That's not what John wanted.

998
01:24:52,554 --> 01:24:53,588
You want a chance?

999
01:24:53,988 --> 01:24:55,223
I'll give you a chance.
